I'm reconsidering whether thread divergence could expose this—if some threads take significantly longer paths, they might desynchronize enough to trigger the race. But the actual kernel logic is too simple: the conditional check on `margin > 0.0f` causes predication within warps rather than true divergence, so all threads in a warp still complete together. Threads beyond index 127 skip the computation entirely and just write zero, which actually finishes faster than the working threads, making any timing advantage work against us.

The reduction's first stride operation has threads 0-127 reading from positions 128-255 in shared memory. Since the padding threads complete their writes before the working threads even reach the synchronization point, the race condition can't manifest under the fixed-shape contract. This mutant appears genuinely resistant to detection.

